Over one time period, \[i(t)=
\begin{cases}
-\dfrac{12t}{T}, &amp; 0\le t\le \dfrac{T}{2},\\ 6, &amp; \dfrac{T}{2}&lt;t\le T.
\end{cases}\] The RMS value is \[I_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\int_{0}^{T}i^2(t)\,dt}.\] Substituting the waveform, \[I_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\left[\int_{0}^{T/2}\left(\frac{12t}{T}\right)^2dt
+\int_{T/2}^{T}6^2\,dt\right]}.\] \[I_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\left[\frac{144}{T^2}\cdot\frac{(T/2)^3}{3}
+36\cdot\frac{T}{2}\right]}
=\sqrt{6+18}
=\sqrt{24}
=2\sqrt{6}\,\text{A}.\] \[\boxed{\text{Option A: }2\sqrt{6}\,\text{A}}\]

The resultant current is \[i(t)=I_{DC}+i_{AC}(t),\] where \[I_{DC}=10\,\text{A}, \qquad I_{AC(\text{peak})}=20\,\text{A}.\] The RMS value of the AC component is \[I_{AC(\mathrm{RMS})}=\frac{20}{\sqrt{2}}=10\sqrt{2}\,\text{A}.\] Since the average value of the AC component over one cycle is zero, \[I_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{I_{DC}^{2}+I_{AC(\mathrm{RMS})}^{2}}.\] Substituting the values, \[I_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{10^{2}+\left(10\sqrt{2}\right)^{2}}
=\sqrt{100+200}
=\sqrt{300}
=10\sqrt{3}
\approx 17.3\,\text{A}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }17.3\,\text{A}}\]

Given: \[Y=(\bar{A}BC+D)(\bar{A}D+\bar{B}\bar{C})\] \[Y=\bar{A}\bar{A}BCD+\bar{A}DD+\bar{A}BC\bar{B}\bar{C}+\bar{B}\bar{C}D\] \[\hspace{5cm}
[\because\; X\cdot\bar{X}=0 \;\text{and}\; X\cdot X=X]\] \[Y=\bar{A}BCD+\bar{A}D+\bar{B}\bar{C}D\] \[Y=\bar{A}D(BC+1)+\bar{B}\bar{C}D\] \[\hspace{4.5cm}
[\because\; 1+BC=1]\] \[Y=\bar{A}D+\bar{B}\bar{C}D\] Hence, the correct option is (A). (A) \(\bar{A}D+\bar{B}\bar{C}D\) <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Two important laws of Boolean algebra: <ol><li>\(1+\) Any literal \(=1\)</li><li>\(0+\) Any literal \(=\) Any literal</li></ol></li></ul></div>

Given characteristic equation: \[s^3 - 4s^2 + s + 6 = 0\] To find the number of roots in the left-half of the \(s\)-plane, we construct the Routh array: \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\
s^2 &amp; -4 &amp; 6 \\
s^1 &amp; \dfrac{(-4)(1) - (6)(1)}{-4} = 2.5 \\
s^0 &amp; 6 &amp; 
\end{array}\] Since there are <strong>two sign changes</strong> in the first column of the Routh array, there are exactly \(2\) roots lying in the right-half of the \(s\)-plane (RHS). Since the highest power of \(s\) is \(3\), the total number of roots is \(3\). Therefore, the remaining number of roots in the left-half of the \(s\)-plane (LHS) is: \[\text{Roots in LHS} = \text{Total roots} - \text{Roots in RHS} = 3 - 2 = 1\] \[\boxed{\text{(B) one}}\]

Given, \[G(s)=\frac{s}{s+1} \qquad \text{and} \qquad x(t)=\sin t\] For a sinusoidal input, the steady-state output is \[y_{ss}(t)=|G(j\omega)|\sin\!\left(\omega t+\angle G(j\omega)\right)\] Here, \(\omega=1\) rad/s. Therefore, \[G(j)=\frac{j}{1+j}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\angle45^\circ\] Hence, the steady-state response is \[y(t)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\sin\!\left(t+45^\circ\right)\] \[\boxed{\text{(B) }\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\sin\!\left(t+45^\circ\right)}\]

Given, \[G(s)=\frac{as+1}{s^2}\] \[G(j\omega)=\frac{1+ja\omega}{(j\omega)^2}
=\frac{\sqrt{1+a^2\omega^2}}{\omega^2}~\angle-180^\circ+\tan^{-1}(a\omega)\] At gain crossover frequency, \[|G(j\omega_{gc})|=1\] \[\frac{\sqrt{1+a^2\omega_{gc}^2}}{\omega_{gc}^2}=1\] \[1+a^2\omega_{gc}^2=\omega_{gc}^4\] Since the phase margin is \(45^\circ\), \[PM=180^\circ+\angle G(j\omega_{gc})\] \[45^\circ
=180^\circ+\left[-180^\circ+\tan^{-1}(a\omega_{gc})\right]\] \[\tan^{-1}(a\omega_{gc})=45^\circ\] \[a\omega_{gc}=1\] \[a=\frac{1}{\omega_{gc}}\] Substituting \(a\omega_{gc}=1\) in \(1+a^2\omega_{gc}^2=\omega_{gc}^4,\) \[1+a^2\omega_{gc}^2=\omega_{gc}^4\] \[1+1=\omega_{gc}^4\] \[\omega_{gc}=2^{1/4}\] \[a=\frac{1}{2^{1/4}}
\approx 0.841\] \[\boxed{\text{(C) }0.841}\]

