For a long air-core solenoid, \[L=\mu_{0}\frac{N^{2}A}{l}\] Given: \[N=3000,\qquad l=1000\,\text{mm}=1\,\text{m}\] \[d=60\,\text{mm}=0.06\,\text{m}\] Hence, \[A=\frac{\pi d^{2}}{4}
=\frac{\pi(0.06)^{2}}{4}
=2.827\times10^{-3}\,\text{m}^{2}\] Substituting, \[L=(4\pi\times10^{-7})
\frac{(3000)^{2}(2.827\times10^{-3})}{1}\] \[L=3.198\times10^{-2}\,\text{H}\] \[L\approx 0.032\,\text{H}
=32\,\text{mH}\] Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{C}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Inductance of a long solenoid: \[L=\mu_{0}\mu_{r}\frac{N^{2}A}{l}\]</li><li>Inductance is proportional to \(N^{2}\), cross-sectional area \(A\), and inversely proportional to length \(l\).</li></ul></div>

