=
\frac{\left|E_{1}-E_{2}\right|}
{Z_{s1}+Z_{s2}}\] Since both induced emfs are in phase, only their magnitudes differ. \[E_{1}
=
\frac{3300}{\sqrt{3}}
\,\text{V}\] \[E_{2}
=
\frac{3200}{\sqrt{3}}
\,\text{V}\] Given, \[Z_{s1}=Z_{s2}=j1.7\,\Omega\] Hence, \[I_{sy}
=
\frac{\dfrac{3300}{\sqrt{3}}-\dfrac{3200}{\sqrt{3}}}
{2\times1.7}\] \[I_{sy}
=
16.98\,\text{A}\] Hence, the correct option is \[\boxed{\text{A}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Unequal excitation of parallel alternators causes circulating current to flow between the machines.</li><li>Circulating current flows even when there is no external load change.</li><li>The synchronizing current depends on the emf difference and the sum of synchronous impedances.</li><li>If the emfs are in phase, only the magnitude difference is considered.</li></ul></div>

