For a capacitor-start induction motor, the auxiliary winding current must lead the main winding current by \(90^\circ\) for maximum starting torque. <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> The impedance of the main winding is \[Z_{m} = (6+j4)\,\Omega\] Hence, \[I_{m}=\frac{V}{Z_{m}}
=\frac{230\angle 0^\circ}{6+j4}
=31.89\angle -33.7^\circ\,\text{A}\] Therefore, the auxiliary winding current should lead by \[90^\circ-33.7^\circ=56.3^\circ\] The auxiliary winding impedance is \[Z_{a}=8+j6-\frac{j}{\omega C}\] Hence, \[\tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{\frac{1}{\omega C}-6}{8}\right)=56.3^\circ\] Substituting \(\omega=2\pi \times 50\) rad/s, \[\frac{\frac{1}{\omega C}-6}{8}
=\tan 56.3^\circ\] \[\frac{1}{\omega C}
=6+8\tan 56.3^\circ\] \[C=\frac{1}{2\pi(50)\left(6+8\tan 56.3^\circ\right)}\] \[C=176.84\,\mu\text{F}\] \[\boxed{A}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Maximum starting torque in a capacitor-start motor is obtained when the main and auxiliary winding currents are in quadrature.</li><li>Main winding current lags due to inductive impedance.</li><li>The capacitor is selected so that the auxiliary winding current leads by the required angle.</li><li>Capacitive reactance is \(X_C=\dfrac{1}{\omega C}\).</li></ul></div>

