The triggering voltage varies as \[V_{b}=12\pm4\,\text{V}\] Hence, \[(V_{b})_{\min}=12-4=8\,\text{V}\] \[(V_{b})_{\max}=12+4=16\,\text{V}\] The thyristor must turn ON even at the minimum triggering voltage. Therefore, the gate resistor is designed using the minimum value of \(V_{b}\). Given, \[I_{g}=10\,\text{mA}\] Neglecting the gate-to-source voltage drop, \[R=\frac{(V_{b})_{\min}}{I_{g}}
=\frac{8}{10\times10^{-3}}
=800\,\Omega\] \[\boxed{D}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The gate resistor is designed using the minimum triggering voltage to ensure reliable firing under worst-case conditions.</li><li>Gate resistance is calculated as \(R=\dfrac{V_{g}}{I_{g}}\) after neglecting the gate-cathode voltage drop (if specified).</li></ul></div>

The average power loss in any switch modeled as a resistance is \[P_{\text{avg}}=I_{\text{rms}}^{2}R\] Given, \[I_{\text{rms}}=\frac{10}{\sqrt{2}}\,\text{A},
\qquad
R=0.15\,\Omega\] Therefore, \[P_{\text{avg}}
=
\left(\frac{10}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^{2}
\times 0.15\] \[P_{\text{avg}}
=
\frac{100\times0.15}{2}
=
7.5\,\text{W}\] \[\boxed{C}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Conduction loss in a resistive switch is calculated using RMS current.</li><li>Use \(P_{\text{avg}}=I_{\text{rms}}^{2}R\).</li></ul></div>
